\par\par \b \uc2  Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  buys dollars as sol rises. \b0\par\par\uc2 212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2002\par 19:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LIMA, Peru, April 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time this year, buying U.S. dollars to prevent too much foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity amid a gradual rise of the sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  . \par \par \uc2  A government source said the bank would announce how many dollars it had bought at 1:30 p.m. (1830 GMT). The last time the bank stepped into the market was in December 2001. \par \par \uc2  "This month, more forward deals are expiring, and the market requires more soles as annual taxes come due, so we are acting to ease the pressure of a greater dollar supply," said the source, who requested anonymity. \par \par \uc2  The bank stepped in as the sol firmed to 3.4390/3.4410 to the dollar by midsession, up from 3.4420/3.4440 at the beginning of the day. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 0.6 percent in recent weeks as companies prepare to pay annual income taxes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Following the bank's dollar purchase, which happened around 12:20 p.m. (1720 GMT), the sol was losing ground at 3.4450/3.4460 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  NOTE: For more information on Peru's sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , please. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020401dy4100vn0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
