\par\par \b \uc2  Peru's Sol Ends Stronger Even As Ctrl Bk Buys Dollars \b0\par\par\uc2 213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2001\par 19:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LIMA -(Dow Jones)- Peru's nuevo sol ended stronger again Tuesday, even as the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second straight day to buy U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The nuevo sol closed at a midrate of PEN3.4425 per dollar on a spread of PEN3.4420-PEN3.4430 per dollar, local traders said. \par \par \uc2  The nuevo sol closed Monday at a midrate of PEN3.4465 per dollar on a spread of PEN3.4460-PEN3.4470 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure on the exchange rate was intense," said one market analyst. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $10 million Tuesday, after buying $5 million on Monday. \par \par \uc2  In recent trading sessions before this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $29.5 million, aiming to slow the appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market analyst have said that the sol has gained strength on weakening imports and an increase in exports of mining products. \par \par \uc2  As of Oct. 29, the key average overnight lending rate was 3.96%, and the outstanding value of CDs, used by the government to control the money supply and influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was PEN1.805 billion, unchanged from the day before. \par \par \uc2  -Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1071 1837 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011030dxau0033o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
